A||||11||fiero56||Electrical problem, can alternator really cause this?||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||06-06-2001||02:46 PM||||'86 SE 2.8 4-speed 105K miles<P>The problem began about a week and a half ago. I discovered my engine was eating belts due to an incorrect alternator pulley. Rather than swap pulleys I took the spare alternator I have from the engine I bought to rebuild. After the alternator swap, the car ran fine until I would be in a parking lot or other low speed driving(higher engine bay temps). The engine would be running fine, then instantly die with no hesitation or warning, and would not start back up. Once it had a chance to cool(several minutes at first progressing to several hours as it got worse), it would fire right up and act like nothing happened, until it warmed up again. I put the old alternator back on, and I have the same problem. I did drop the old alternator a couple of times, could this cause it. Can a bad alternator cause this condition? I have replaced ignition coil and ignition module. Pick-up coil is next on my list, but can an alternator cause this problem? Thanks for any help. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Steve Normington||06-06-2001||03:03 PM||||IMHO, an alternator could not cause this problem unless the alternator shorts and drains the battery current. If that were to happen, you'd likely melt the wiring and it wouldn't start again. Even if the alternator completely fails, there would be enough battery current to run the car for a long time. I think your on the right track with the ignition componants as the problem. Also, if the fuel pump is dying, the increased temperature may be giving you vapor lock.||||reg||1
Z||000002||starfighter007@msn.com||06-06-2001||05:44 PM||||It sounds likea fuel pump problem like when it  gets hot it quits pumping I suppose thats easy enough to check with a presure gauge I was on a long road trip from chicago to phx driving about 80to 85 mph.most of the useing a lot of gas for a small fuel tank I'm not sure but I dont think the fuel pump cares very much for thee fuel tank to go below 1/4 tank for long periods of time especially in warm weather any ways before I got to alb.new mexico it quit working till it cooled off thenit would work then quit after maybe 5 to ten min.well you get the picture. so after it dies check your fuel presure good luck||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||06-06-2001||06:27 PM||||Fuel pump is brand new, and I only have 20 miles on this tank of gas. I think if the fuel pump were letting go, the engine wouldn't stop so abruptly. I don't even have to to react to try and prevent the stall. Its dead before I get a chance to hit the throttle, which makes me think its something with ignition.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||06-06-2001||07:40 PM||||Since the pump it new... ignition module, pickup, or coil. Next time it dies see if it's got spark....||||reg||1
