Z||000006||fiero56||06-06-2001||08:41 PM||||Latest update.<P>The car will not start, even though it has been sitting at least 6 hours. Around 1pm, I started it up, and let it run until it died. It took about 5 minutes before it died instantly as usual, and would not start again. I am not getting any spark from ignition coil. I took the dist cap off, cleaned all contacts, made sure all my connections were good, and it made no difference.<BR>This same thing happened with the other alternator, one day it just wouldn't fire. After that, I put in the original alternator, and it fired up, but wouldn't run more than a few minutes at a time. That is the alternator in right now. I'm thinking the alternator is all that's left, but its a $90 mistake if I'm wrong.<P>I have also run through every wire near the fuseable links. I have double checked every connection to the alternator, and such. I am also not getting a signal to the tach(no possibility the tach filter is bad either), which means no signal is leaving the dist. But I wonder if the distributor is getting any juice to begin with?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 06-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||DJRice||06-06-2001||08:59 PM||||Your local parts house can test that coil for you.  Its highly likely that your coil is the problem.  I suffered the exact same symptoms two weeks ago, and I ended up having the car towed two blocks to my apartment because I didnt want to leave it by itself in the area it died (at night).  <BR>$25 should get you back in the business.||||reg||1
Z||000008||PBJ||06-06-2001||09:10 PM||||I agree with DJRice,  pickup coil and ignition module is not usually intermittent. It stays broken.  But a coil comes and goes.  Even if you test it, it may turn out ok until hot.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fierowrecker||06-06-2001||10:34 PM||||Hey 56!<BR>Look carefully at the alt wiring...<BR>Specificly, the bat wire...<BR>I had a splice in that wire half way to the battery, where it split into two wires...<BR>The splice was bare metal, with black tape wrapped around it, but it came off allowing the splice to short...<BR>It also would cause the car to die...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||06-06-2001||10:56 PM||||I replaced the pick-up coil with a known good one, and it fired up. I am still not sure if this is the final fix or not, but it starts and runs, without any stalling once it heats up. I don't have headlights in the car at the moment, so won't get to take it out until tommorrow.<P>This never started until I swapped alternators, and seemed to change conditions everytime I switched the alternators, which made me think they were the cause, since the problems started the day I switched alts. <P>I changed the alternators so many times, I can now swap alts and tension the serp belt in about 10 minutes with no help.||||reg||1
Z||000011||maryjane||06-06-2001||11:54 PM||||This is what my 84 was doing, after driving about thirty minutes, if we shut it off, it would not start unless, I poured a little gas in the throttle body. I wrapped the fuel filter and line in some insulation and it's been fine. This doesn't sound like your problem since I think you said it was v-6. I have seen injectors on diesels have a bad check valve and let combustion gas go into<BR>the fuel line & have heard of it in gasoline fuel injected systems. The air just compresses and won't allow the fuel to reach the injectors. I'm not real familar with gasoline injector systems. Just a stab in the dark tho.<BR>Don||||reg||5
